 ## Overview
-Geometry Shader Cube Expansion is a technique that converts volumetric data or mesh surfaces into geometric primitives by expanding input points into full cube shells within the geometry shader [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering voxel-based volumes or shell-voxelized meshes with minimal CPU-side geometry preparation by generating the 6 faces of a cube on the GPU from a single point primitive [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. Unlike [Geometry Shader (Quads)](4369-geometry-shader-quads.md), which may break voxels down to individual faces or use display lists, this method generates up to 6 quads per voxel point, offering a balance between memory efficiency and render speed [S2, S3, unofficial]. It builds on [Geometry Shader Processing](2524-geometry-shader-processing.md) by utilizing the shader's ability to emit multiple primitives from a single input.
+Geometry Shader Cube Expansion is a technique that converts mesh surfaces or pre-computed voxel data into geometric primitives by expanding input points into full cube shells within the geometry shader [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering voxel-based volumes or shell-voxelized meshes with minimal CPU-side geometry preparation by generating the 6 faces of a cube on the GPU from a single point primitive [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. Unlike [Geometry Shader (Quads)](4369-geometry-shader-quads.md), which may break voxels down to individual faces or use display lists, this method generates up to 6 quads per voxel point, offering a balance between memory efficiency and render speed [S2, S3, unofficial]. It builds on [Geometry Shader Processing](2524-geometry-shader-processing.md) by utilizing the shader's ability to emit multiple primitives from a single input [G].
 
 ## How it works
-The method operates in two primary modes depending on the input source: mesh-based voxelization (centroid expansion) and pre-computed voxel data (flag-based expansion). Both modes rely on the geometry shader to reconstruct 3D geometry from 1D point inputs.
+The method operates in two primary modes depending on the input source: mesh-based voxelization (centroid expansion) and pre-computed voxel data (flag-based expansion) [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. Both modes rely on the geometry shader to reconstruct 3D geometry from 1D point inputs [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
 
 ### Inputs and Outputs
-*   **Input (Mesh Mode):** A stream of triangles (`GL_TRIANGLES`). The vertex shader computes the centroid of each triangle.
-*   **Input (Voxel Mode):** A stream of points (`GL_POINTS`). Each point contains the voxel center position, color, and a bitmask of enabled faces [S2, S3, unofficial].
-*   **Output:** A stream of triangle strips (`GL_TRIANGLE_STRIP`). In mesh mode, `max_vertices` is 24 (6 faces × 4 vertices). In voxel mode, `max_vertices` is 12 (optimized to emit only visible faces) [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
+*   **Input (Mesh Mode):** A stream of triangles (`GL_TRIANGLES`) processed by the geometry shader [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Input (Voxel Mode):** A stream of points (`GL_POINTS`) where each point contains the voxel center position, color, and a bitmask of enabled faces [S2, S3, unofficial].
+*   **Output:** A stream of triangle strips (`GL_TRIANGLE_STRIP`) with `max_vertices` set to 24 for mesh mode or 12 for voxel mode [S1, S2, S3, unofficial].
 
